 ### Barycentric Feature Interpolation for Neural Textures
-A sampling technique for neural proxy volume rendering that maps Cartesian coordinates to Barycentric coordinates to sample features from a neural texture, using local information instead of global scene coordinates [S6, unofficial]. It decomposes proxy prisms into three tetrahedra using the method from [PBFJ05]. For a Cartesian point $x$ and tetrahedron $\tau$ with vertices $ABCD$, it computes volumes $V_A, V_B, V_C, V_D$ and $V_{ABCD}$ to derive barycentric coordinates $\phi_A, \phi_B, \phi_C, \phi_D$. It checks if sample $x$ is within tetrahedron $\tau$ by verifying $\phi_A, \phi_B, \phi_C, \phi_D \ge 0$. It finds the specific tetrahedron $\tau_x$ containing $x$. It computes UV coordinate $u$ from Barycentric coordinates using vertex UVs: $u = \phi_A u_A + \phi_B u_B + \phi_C u_C + \phi_D u_D$ [S6, unofficial]. Parameters include $\theta_{Tex}$ for the learned network $S_{Tex}$ and $\theta_p$ for the proxy sampler $\hat{S}_p$ [S6, unofficial].
